<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Exeter</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isca Dumnoniorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Exeter is a cathedral city on the River Exe and the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began around 55 CE as a fortress of the Roman legio II Augusta, and when the army moved on it became Isca Dumnoniorum, the civitas capital of the Dumnonii. It later became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> burh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> in a siege of 1068.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/exeter.jpg" alt="Exeter" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exeter_Cathedral,_Devon_213.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Exeter stands on the eastern bank of the River Exe, on a ridge backed by a steep hill, at the point where the river, just joined by the River Creedy, opens onto a wide flood plain and estuary. Historically this was the lowest bridging point of the Exe, which was tidal and navigable up to the city until weirs were built. Its name comes from the Old English Escanceaster, the river's name with the suffix -ceaster, used for fortresses and fortified towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Coins from the Hellenistic kingdoms suggest a settlement trading with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> as early as 250 BCE. About 55 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> established a fort of 42 acres here, named Isca, which was the south-western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a> and the base of the 5,000-man <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">Second Augustan Legion</a>. A community of natives and soldiers' families grew up around it as the tribal capital of the Dumnonii. When the fortress was abandoned about 75, its large bathhouse was replaced by a forum and a basilica.</p><p>Little is known of Exeter between the Roman withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> around 410 and the seventh century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> held it. Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> briefly captured it in 876, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> drove them out the next summer. He made it one of the four burhs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> and rebuilt its walls on the Roman lines, which let it repel a siege in 893. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> strengthened the walls about 928 and drove out the remaining Britons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> plundered the city in 1003, let in by the French reeve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>, who had been given Exeter as part of her dowry.</p><p>Exeter was made a county corporate in 1537. In 1549 it withstood a month-long siege by the Prayer Book rebels, who burnt two of its gates and tried to undermine its walls before they were defeated by the army of Edward VI. It became an affluent centre of the wool trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> largely bypassed it for lack of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, and in 1778 a new bridge replaced the 500-year-old Old Exe Bridge. Much of the city centre was rebuilt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Exeter is a cathedral city and the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, with Exeter Cathedral founded in the mid 11th century. It is home to the University of Exeter, which received its royal charter in 1955. The Exeter Book, a codex of Old English poetry, is kept in the cathedral. The city has over 1,800 listed buildings, many medieval, Tudor and Georgian, and was made a UNESCO City of Literature in 2019. The Exeter Ship Canal, first built about 1566, is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s oldest artificial waterways.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Exeter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within four years a frontier ran roughly along the line later followed by the Fosse Way, from <b>Exeter</b> to Lincoln.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman forces reached the south-west after 43, and around 55 the legio II Augusta built a fortress at <b>Exeter</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li></ul></section>
